00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.655
And the first question is why are we asking you to buy plow truck?

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:12.895
So we, as a town in 2018,

00:03:12.895 --> 00:03:14.895
did a pretty exhaustive study

00:03:15.055 --> 00:03:20.495
about what the cheapest way to supply plow trucks to the town of Andover is.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:25.240
We looked at a bunch of other states and a bunch of other municipalities

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.840
and we came to, like, four main conclusions.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:36.695
The first is if we hold plow trucks longer than their optimal life, it costs us more than if we don't. So

00:03:36.855 --> 00:03:39.015
using something for thirty years

00:03:39.175 --> 00:03:45.015
when for the last ten, your maintenance costs are for the roof is just not an economical thing for the town to do.

00:03:45.415 --> 00:03:49.760
The second thing is that the biggest thing that kills us is rust.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.280
The third is that there's a big difference in manufacturers

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.840
in the total cost of ownership,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.920
and buying a less expensive

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.680
vehicle up front

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.555
in the past a lot more money overall than spending the money upfront

00:04:06.555 --> 00:04:08.635
to get a better quality vehicle.

00:04:09.035 --> 00:04:12.795
And then the fourth conclusion is that the body rot

00:04:12.875 --> 00:04:16.155
accounts for an awful lot of the cost and maintenance of these vehicles.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:18.690
So

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:19.890
in

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.050
2018,

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:23.330
the CIP

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:28.450
approved and instituted a fifteen year equipment plan for public works

00:04:28.965 --> 00:04:31.125
And the goal was right here,

00:04:31.365 --> 00:04:35.685
to balance the cost, productivity, and reliability of the equipment.

00:04:36.565 --> 00:04:40.325
And we looked at basically everything that public works had.

00:04:40.645 --> 00:04:47.510
And one of things the that came out of it was that plow trucks are the single most expensive asset in public works.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:54.470
So here's what we're trying to do to maximize the lifespan and minimize cost.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.085
First is we purchase one truck every fifth year.

00:04:58.485 --> 00:05:00.485
We have three primary

00:05:00.485 --> 00:05:12.325
plow routes and that means each truck is used for fifteen years as a primary plow truck and then it goes into a reserve or a spare role for another five years. So we're anticipating,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:19.570
you know, the the most cost effective way to do it is to use these trucks for about twenty years total.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.970
The second thing is

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:28.265
with the last truck we bought and with this one, we've switched from regular mild steel bodies

00:05:28.505 --> 00:05:29.065
to

00:05:29.625 --> 00:05:32.345
hardox slash stainless steel bodies.

00:05:32.825 --> 00:05:39.380
And we did this because we were getting about twelve years out of the old mild steel bodies.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.660
Today, we were to rebody a truck.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:46.260
It would cost us around $45,000

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:51.140
to buy the body and it's about another 20,000 to do the install,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.220
presuming we don't have hydraulics.

00:05:53.665 --> 00:05:56.705
So if you spend an extra $25,000

00:05:56.785 --> 00:05:58.625
to buy a stainless body

00:05:58.705 --> 00:06:02.945
and you don't have to replace it, overall, you save the town quite a bit of money.

00:06:03.665 --> 00:06:09.010
The second thing is that all of these trucks get fluid film undercoated

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.090
every year before plow season.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:15.170
Because one of the best things you can do is to prevent corrosion,

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.090
so they get basically,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.915
completely washed and fluid coated every year.

00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:29.515
The second thing is after every storm, every bit of salt gets unloaded from these vehicles and the vehicles get washed.

00:06:29.675 --> 00:06:34.040
And if we have looks like we have a break storms, we usually power wash em.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:40.600
Again, we're doing everything we can to prevent corrosion in the vehicles so we can run em as long as we can.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:42.440
Now, with that,

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:46.520
we're still generally planning around year ten for these vehicles

00:06:46.985 --> 00:06:50.745
of bringing them in, having the frames and bodies sandblasted

00:06:50.745 --> 00:06:51.865
and repainted.

00:06:51.945 --> 00:06:58.425
Again, the idea is to do lower cost items to extend the lives of them as much as possible.

00:06:59.865 --> 00:07:03.830
So, this is what we have right now. You can see we're

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.390
